steps.body:
A trie stores words as paths down from a root node, one letter per step. Each node has up to 26 children, one per lowercase letter, kept in an array indexed by `c - 'a'`, and a flag that says a word ends there. Words that share a prefix share its nodes:

```cpp
struct TrieNode {
    TrieNode* children[26] = {};   // all nullptr until a word uses that letter
    bool isEnd = false;            // a word ends at this node

    TrieNode() = default;
    TrieNode(const TrieNode&) = delete;
    TrieNode& operator=(const TrieNode&) = delete;
    ~TrieNode() {
        for (TrieNode* child : children) {
            delete child;          // deleting nullptr does nothing
        }
    }
};

void insert(TrieNode& root, const std::string& word) {
    TrieNode* node = &root;
    for (char c : word) {
        int i = c - 'a';
        if (node->children[i] == nullptr) {
            node->children[i] = new TrieNode();
        }
        node = node->children[i];
    }
    node->isEnd = true;
}

TrieNode root;
insert(root, "app");
insert(root, "apple");
std::cout << std::boolalpha << root.children['a' - 'a']->isEnd << '\n';   // false, "a" is only a prefix
```

Each node owns its children, so its destructor deletes them, and the whole trie frees itself when `root` goes out of scope. Copying a node would give the same children two owners and delete them twice, so the copy operations are deleted, as in the Constructors, Destructors, and RAII lesson. Declaring them removes the free default constructor, so `TrieNode() = default;` asks for it back. The `= {}` matters too. Without it, a node made as `TrieNode root;` would start with garbage pointers. `new TrieNode()` happens to zero them through its `()`, but `= {}` makes every node safe however it is made.

To search, walk the same path from the root. A missing child means the word isn't there. Reaching the last letter only proves the text is a prefix of some word, so the answer is that node's `isEnd`.

