Give each node a `count` of the words that pass through it, and add 1 to every node on the path as you insert: `node = node->children[i]; ++node->count;`. Then the node at the end of a prefix knows how many words start with it. Both prefix questions begin by walking to that node:

```cpp harness
struct TrieNode { TrieNode* children[26] = {}; bool isEnd = false; int count = 0; TrieNode() = default; TrieNode(const TrieNode&) = delete; TrieNode& operator=(const TrieNode&) = delete; ~TrieNode() { for (TrieNode* c : children) delete c; } };
void insert(TrieNode& root, const std::string& w) { TrieNode* n = &root; for (char c : w) { int i = c - 'a'; if (!n->children[i]) n->children[i] = new TrieNode(); n = n->children[i]; ++n->count; } n->isEnd = true; }
{{code}}
TrieNode root;
for (const char* w : {"apple", "app", "apply", "bat"}) {
    insert(root, w);
}
const TrieNode* node = walk(root, "app");
std::cout << node->count << ' ' << node->isEnd << ' ' << (walk(root, "cat") == nullptr) << '\n';
```

```cpp
const TrieNode* walk(const TrieNode& root, const std::string& prefix) {
    const TrieNode* node = &root;
    for (char c : prefix) {
        node = node->children[c - 'a'];
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;   // no word starts with this prefix
        }
    }
    return node;
}
```

`walk(root, p) != nullptr` answers "does any word start with `p`?", `node->count` says how many do, and `node->isEnd` says whether the prefix is a word itself. Every query costs one step per letter of the prefix, however many words the trie holds. If the same word can be inserted twice, it counts twice, so check `contains` first when each word should count once.

