steps.body:
Autocomplete lists every word under a prefix. Walk to the prefix's node, then explore everything below it depth first, carrying the letters so far in a string. It is the backtracking from the Recursion and Depth-First Search lesson: push a letter, recurse, pop it:

```cpp harness
struct TrieNode { TrieNode* children[26] = {}; bool isEnd = false; int count = 0; TrieNode() = default; TrieNode(const TrieNode&) = delete; TrieNode& operator=(const TrieNode&) = delete; ~TrieNode() { for (TrieNode* c : children) delete c; } };
void insert(TrieNode& root, const std::string& w) { TrieNode* n = &root; for (char c : w) { int i = c - 'a'; if (!n->children[i]) n->children[i] = new TrieNode(); n = n->children[i]; ++n->count; } n->isEnd = true; }
const TrieNode* walk(const TrieNode& root, const std::string& p) { const TrieNode* n = &root; for (char c : p) { n = n->children[c - 'a']; if (!n) return nullptr; } return n; }
{{code}}
TrieNode root;
for (const char* w : {"card", "care", "cat", "car", "dog"}) {
    insert(root, w);
}
std::string path = "car";
std::vector<std::string> out;
collect(walk(root, path), path, out);
for (const std::string& w : out) {
    std::cout << w << ' ';
}
std::cout << '\n';
```

```cpp
void collect(const TrieNode* node, std::string& path, std::vector<std::string>& out) {
    if (node->isEnd) {
        out.push_back(path);           // path spells a whole word
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < 26; ++i) {
        if (node->children[i] != nullptr) {
            path.push_back(static_cast<char>('a' + i));
            collect(node->children[i], path, out);
            path.pop_back();
        }
    }
}
```

Start `path` as the prefix itself, so every word comes out whole. A node's own word goes in before its children's, and the children go from `a` to `z`, so the words come out sorted with no `std::sort`: `car`, then `card`, then `care`. The work is the size of the trie below the prefix, not the whole dictionary. To stop after a few suggestions, check the size of `out` in the loop's condition, before each recursive call.

