steps.body:
In a pattern like `b.d`, a dot stands for any one letter. A trie answers that with a depth-first search: for a letter, follow its one child, but for a dot, try every child and succeed if any of them matches the rest of the pattern:

```cpp harness
struct TrieNode { TrieNode* children[26] = {}; bool isEnd = false; int count = 0; TrieNode() = default; TrieNode(const TrieNode&) = delete; TrieNode& operator=(const TrieNode&) = delete; ~TrieNode() { for (TrieNode* c : children) delete c; } };
void insert(TrieNode& root, const std::string& w) { TrieNode* n = &root; for (char c : w) { int i = c - 'a'; if (!n->children[i]) n->children[i] = new TrieNode(); n = n->children[i]; ++n->count; } n->isEnd = true; }
{{code}}
TrieNode root;
for (const char* w : {"bad", "dad", "mad"}) {
    insert(root, w);
}
std::cout << std::boolalpha << match(&root, ".ad", 0) << ' ' << match(&root, "b.", 0) << '\n';
```

```cpp
bool match(const TrieNode* node, const std::string& pattern, std::size_t i) {
    if (node == nullptr) {
        return false;                  // fell off the trie
    }
    if (i == pattern.size()) {
        return node->isEnd;            // used every letter: is it a whole word?
    }
    if (pattern[i] == '.') {
        for (const TrieNode* child : node->children) {
            if (match(child, pattern, i + 1)) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
    return match(node->children[pattern[i] - 'a'], pattern, i + 1);
}
```

Start with `match(&root, pattern, 0)`. Checking for `nullptr` first lets every call pass a child along without testing it. A letter costs one step, and each dot can multiply the work by up to 26, which is still far less than checking every word when the dots are few. The `for` loop over `node->children` visits all 26 slots, empty ones included, and the `nullptr` check turns those away.

